    "summary": "This PR restructures the agent-config repo into a shared `src/user/.agents/` tree plus per-tool directories (`.claude`, `.codex`, `.gemini`) and rewrites `scripts/install.sh` into a multi-tool installer with auto-detection, a Bash 4 guard, per-tool counters, and a `--tools=` override. Most of the diff is file renames and new templates; the reviewable logic is concentrated in `scripts/install.sh` (template sync, directory sync, JSON union-merge / TOML copy, tool detection). The visible hunks look largely correct and actually fix a prior flaw where `--dry-run` still prompted with `confirm`, but the argument handling and dry-run accounting have small behavioral gaps. Reviewers should also inspect the truncated tail of `install.sh` (per-tool summary and main install loop) and confirm that the new `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE-EXTENSIONS.md` reference chain resolves in Claude Code, which the author lists as unverified.",
    "risk_level": "low",
    "rule_findings": [],
    "suggestions": [
      {
        "file_path": "scripts/install.sh",
        "line": 129,
        "severity": "low",
        "confidence": 0.7,
        "title": "Trim whitespace when parsing the --tools list",
        "reason": "The new override parsing splits the value on commas only (`IFS=',' read -ra TOOLS <<< \"$TOOLS_OVERRIDE\"`), and the validation loop immediately after rejects any element that is not an exact match for an entry in `ALL_TOOLS` (`err \"Unknown tool: $tool (valid: ${ALL_TOOLS[*]})\"`). A space after a comma — the natural way to write a comma-separated CLI list — therefore produces a hard failure instead of being tolerated.",
        "recommendation": "Normalize each entry before validating, e.g. inside the validation loop do `tool=\"${tool//[[:space:]]/}\"` (and also strip when building `TOOLS`), or change the parse to `IFS=', ' read -ra TOOLS <<< \"$TOOLS_OVERRIDE\"`.",
        "source": "ai",
        "evidence": [
          "IFS=',' read -ra TOOLS <<< \"$TOOLS_OVERRIDE\""
        ],
        "failure_scenario": "Running `scripts/install.sh --yes --tools=\"claude, codex\"` yields `x Unknown tool:  codex (valid: claude codex gemini)` and exits with status 1, installing nothing, even though both requested tool names are valid."
      }
    ],
